# Comments

Do **not** sprinkle comments through the codebase by default. Prefer clear names,
small functions, and numpydoc on public APIs over inline narration.

## When to comment

- Non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or constraints that the code alone cannot show
- Subtle bugs / edge cases (why a guard exists, why an ordering matters)
- Workarounds for upstream / framework quirks
- `# noqa` / type ignores only with a short justification when required

## When not to comment

- Restating what the next line obviously does
- Section banners (`# --- helpers ---`) or changelog-style notes
- TODOs that belong in an issue / PR description
- Docstring duplication immediately above the same logic

```python
# BAD
# Get the user name from the environment
user = os.environ.get("USER", "friend")

# GOOD (no comment — the code is clear)
user = os.environ.get("USER", "friend")

# GOOD (comment earns its place)
# LlamaIndex / pypdf often use 0-based page; keep label as-is if >= 1
page = raw_page + 1 if raw_page == 0 else raw_page
```

If a comment is needed, keep it one short line and explain **why**, not **what**.
